For maximum power transfer, first find the Thevenin equivalent seen by \(R\). <strong>Thevenin Voltage \(V_{th}\):</strong><br/>Remove \(R\) and find the open-circuit voltage across its terminals. Transform the \(2\) A current source in parallel with \(5\Omega\) into an equivalent voltage source: \[V=IR=(2)(5)=10~\text{V}\] <img src="img/927e8688185c7771.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> Applying KVL, \[-5+5I-6+5I-10=0\] \[10I=21\] \[I=2.1~\text{A}\] Also, \[V_{th}=5-5I\] \[V_{th}=5-5(2.1)=-5.5~\text{V}\] \[|V_{th}|=5.5~\text{V}\] <strong>Thevenin Resistance \(R_{th}\):</strong><br/>Deactivate all independent sources: <img src="img/8f7c38aad675e49f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> The \(3\Omega\) resistor gets short-circuited by the deactivated \(6\) V source. Hence, \[R_{th}=5\parallel5\] \[R_{th}
=
\frac{5\times5}{5+5}
=
2.5~\Omega\] Therefore, the maximum power transferred to \(R\) is \[P_{\max}
=
\frac{V_{th}^2}{4R_{th}}\] \[P_{\max}
=
\frac{(5.5)^2}{4(2.5)}=
3.025~\text{W}\] \[\boxed{3.025}\]

The poles of the causal and stable system are \[p_k=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\exp\!\left(j\frac{(2k-1)\pi}{4}\right),\qquad k=1,2,3,4,\] and all the zeros are at \(z=0\). Since all four zeros are at the origin, \[H(z)=K\frac{z^{4}}{\prod\limits_{k=1}^{4}(z-p_k)}.\] The poles are \[\frac{1+j}{2},\;
\frac{-1+j}{2},\;
\frac{-1-j}{2},\;
\frac{1-j}{2}.\] Hence, \[\prod_{k=1}^{4}(z-p_k)
=\left(z^2-z+\frac12\right)\left(z^2+z+\frac12\right)
=z^4+\frac14.\] Therefore, \[H(z)=K\frac{z^4}{z^4+\frac14}
=\frac{K}{1+\frac14z^{-4}}.\] Using \[H(1)=\frac54,\] we get \[\frac{K}{1+\frac14}=\frac54
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
K=\frac{25}{16}.\] Thus, \[H(z)
=\frac{\frac{25}{16}}{1+\frac14z^{-4}}
=\frac{25}{16}\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\left(-\frac14\right)^mz^{-4m}.\] Hence, \[h[n]
=\frac{25}{16}\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\left(-\frac14\right)^m\delta[n-4m].\] Since \[g[n]=j^nh[n],\] at \(n=8\), \[h[8]
=\frac{25}{16}\left(-\frac14\right)^2
=\frac{25}{256},\] and \[j^8=1.\] Therefore, \[g[8]=\frac{25}{256}=0.09765625.\] \[\boxed{0.098}\]

For an LTI system with zero initial conditions, \[\frac{d^2y(t)}{dt^2}+7\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+10y(t)=4x(t)+5\frac{dx(t)}{dt}\] Taking Laplace transform, \[(s^2+7s+10)Y(s)=(4+5s)X(s)\] Hence, the transfer function is \[H(s)=\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}
=\frac{5s+4}{(s+2)(s+5)}.\] Using partial fraction expansion, \[\frac{5s+4}{(s+2)(s+5)}
=\frac{A}{s+2}+\frac{B}{s+5}\] \[5s+4=A(s+5)+B(s+2).\] Putting \(s=-2\), \[-6=3A \;\Rightarrow\; A=-2.\] Putting \(s=-5\), \[-21=-3B \;\Rightarrow\; B=7.\] Therefore, \[H(s)=\frac{-2}{s+2}+\frac{7}{s+5}.\] Taking inverse Laplace transform, \[h(t)=\left(-2e^{-2t}+7e^{-5t}\right)u(t).\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }-2e^{-2t}u(t)+7e^{-5t}u(t)}\]

