Given synchronous sequential circuit features two distinct shift register configurations driven by a common clock: <img src="img/0b5e156856357b50.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> <strong>1. Analyzing Register Configurations &amp; Next-State Equations:</strong><br/><ul><li><strong>Top Register (\(Q_A, Q_B, Q_C\)):</strong> The serial input to the first stage (\(D_A\)) is fed back directly from the true output of the last stage (\(Q_C\)). This forms a <strong>Ring Counter</strong> (period = 3). \[\begin{aligned}
        Q_{A, n+1} &amp;= Q_{C, n}, \quad Q_{B, n+1} = Q_{A, n}, \quad Q_{C, n+1} = Q_{B, n}
    \end{aligned}\]</li><li><strong>Bottom Register (\(Q_A', Q_B', Q_C'\)):</strong> The serial input to the first stage (\(D_A'\)) is fed back from the complementary output of the last stage (\(\bar{Q}_C'\)). This forms a <strong>Johnson Counter / Twisted Ring Counter</strong> (period = \(2 \times 3 = 6\)). \[\begin{aligned}
        Q_{A, n+1}' &amp;= \bar{Q}_{C, n}', \quad Q_{B, n+1}' = Q_{A, n}', \quad Q_{C, n+1}' = Q_{B, n}'
    \end{aligned}\]</li></ul> <strong>2. Output Logic Expression:</strong><br/>The combinational logic consists of three 2-input XOR gates feeding into a 3-input OR gate: \[Z = (Q_A \oplus Q_A') + (Q_B \oplus Q_B') + (Q_C \oplus Q_C')\] For the output \(Z\) to be \(0\), the output of every individual XOR gate must be \(0\). This implies that the states of both counters must match bit-for-bit: \[Z = 0 \iff (Q_A = Q_A') \text{ AND } (Q_B = Q_B') \text{ AND } (Q_C = Q_C')\] <strong>3. State Transition Table Tracking:</strong><br/>Starting with the given initial conditions \(Q_A Q_B Q_C = 100\) and \(Q_A' Q_B' Q_C' = 100\): <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td><strong>Clock Cycle</strong></td><td>\(Q_A\)</td><td>\(Q_B\)</td><td>\(Q_C\)</td><td>\(Q_A'\)</td><td>\(Q_B'\)</td><td>\(Q_C'\)</td><td>\(Q_A \oplus Q_A'\)</td><td>\(Q_B \oplus Q_B'\)</td><td>\(Q_C \oplus Q_C'\)</td><td><strong>Output (\(Z\))</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Initially (0)</strong></td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr></table></div> As shown in the state sequence trace, the states match identically again after precisely \(6\) clock cycles. <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A 3-bit standard ring counter repeats its sequence every \(3\) clock cycles.</li><li>A 3-bit Johnson counter repeats its sequence every \(2N = 6\) clock cycles.</li><li>The least common multiple (LCM) of their individual periods (\(LCM(3, 6) = 6\)) determines the minimum interval at which their initial aligned state configuration repeats.</li></ul></div> Hence, the minimum number of clock cycles after which the output \(Z\) would again become zero is <strong>6</strong>. <strong>6</strong>

