Let the admittance of the series branch be \[y=\frac{1}{Z}\] The transformer has a complex turns ratio: \[1:\alpha\] where \(\alpha\) has non-zero real and imaginary parts. Let bus voltages and injected currents be: \[V_1,\;V_2\] and \[I_1,\;I_2\] For the ideal transformer, \[V'=\frac{V_2}{\alpha}\] Applying KCL at Bus-\(1\), \[I_1=y(V_1-V')\] Substituting, \[I_1
=
y\left(V_1-\frac{V_2}{\alpha}\right)\] \[I_1
=
yV_1-\frac{y}{\alpha}V_2\] For Bus-\(2\) current, \[I_2
=
-\frac{I_1}{\alpha^*}\] Substituting the expression for \(I_1\), \[I_2
=
-\frac{y}{\alpha^*}V_1
+
\frac{y}{|\alpha|^2}V_2\] Hence, \[\begin{bmatrix}
I_1\\
I_2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
y &amp; -\dfrac{y}{\alpha}\\ -\dfrac{y}{\alpha^*} &amp; \dfrac{y}{|\alpha|^2}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_1\\
V_2
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[Y_{bus}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
y &amp; -\dfrac{y}{\alpha}\\ -\dfrac{y}{\alpha^*} &amp; \dfrac{y}{|\alpha|^2}
\end{bmatrix}\] Now, \[Y_{12}=-\frac{y}{\alpha}\] and \[Y_{21}=-\frac{y}{\alpha^*}\] Since \(\alpha\) is complex with non-zero imaginary part, \[\alpha\neq\alpha^*\] Hence, \[Y_{12}\neq Y_{21}\] Therefore, \(Y_{bus}\) is unsymmetric. Also, \[Z_{bus}=Y_{bus}^{-1}\] The inverse of an unsymmetric matrix is also unsymmetric. Hence, \(Z_{bus}\) is unsymmetric. \[\boxed{\text{(D) Both }Y_{bus}\text{ and }Z_{bus}\text{ are unsymmetric}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A <strong>phase-shifting</strong> transformer with <strong>complex</strong> tap ratio introduces <strong>asymmetry</strong> in the bus admittance matrix.</li><li>Symmetry condition requires: \[Y_{12}=Y_{21}\]</li><li>If \(\alpha\) is complex, \[\alpha\neq\alpha^*\] which destroys reciprocity and symmetry.</li><li>The inverse of an unsymmetric matrix generally remains unsymmetric.</li></ul></div>

